## 1. Introduction

**${project_name_short}** is a unified source port for id Tech 3 games and for
the Ritual engine that grew from it. One distribution -- one client, one
dedicated server and three renderer modules -- aims to run ten games:

- **Quake III Arena** -- 1.32 and ioquake3 online, the original 1.29 to 1.31
  engines' servers and clients, and recordings back to 1.11 -- the
  February 1999 IHV Test and the public Q3Test releases of 1999;
- **Quake Live**;
- **Return to Castle Wolfenstein**, single player and multiplayer;
- **Wolfenstein: Enemy Territory**;
- **Star Trek Voyager: Elite Force**, single player and Holomatch;
- **Star Wars Jedi Knight: Jedi Academy**, single player and multiplayer;
- **Star Wars Jedi Knight II: Jedi Outcast**, single player and multiplayer;
- **American McGee's Alice** and **Heavy Metal: F.A.K.K.2**, which run
  Ritual Entertainment's engine rather than a Quake III one;
- **Medal of Honor: Allied Assault**, whose engine 2015 built from Ritual's
  and Quake III's.

At startup the engine selects one **game profile** -- the contracts of one
game or game mode -- before the filesystem, network, game modules, renderer or
any optional service starts, and it keeps that profile until it exits. Each
game keeps what its original engine promised: its module interfaces, network
protocol, demos, saves, file formats and gameplay. How the engine *presents*
the game is fnTech3's own. The same renderers, spatial audio, input, console
and screenshot tools serve every game, fed with that game's own data.

fnTech3 grew from two engines: FnQuake3's modernization of Quake III and
FnQL's Quake Live compatibility work, both descended from Quake3e, ioquake3 and
id Software's source release.

### Games and profiles

| Game | Profiles | Status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Quake III Arena | `quake3` (`q3`) | **Enabled** in every build: 1.32 (protocol 68) and ioquake3 (71), 1.29 to 1.31 servers and clients (66, 67), and recordings from 1.11 on ([versions](docs/GAMES.md#quake-iii-arena-quake3)). |
| Quake Live | `quakelive` (`ql`) | **Enabled** in every build; the client runs on Windows x86, where the retail client modules run. |
| Return to Castle Wolfenstein | `rtcw-sp`, `rtcw-mp` | Experimental: developer builds with `-Drtcw-development=true`. |
| Wolfenstein: Enemy Territory | `wolfet` (`et`) | Experimental: `-Dwolfet-development=true`. |
| Star Trek Voyager: Elite Force | `stvef-sp`, `stvef-mp` | Experimental: `-Dstvef-development=true`. |
| Star Wars Jedi Knight: Jedi Academy | `jka-sp`, `jka-mp` | Experimental, dedicated server only: `-Djka-development=true`. |
| Star Wars Jedi Knight II: Jedi Outcast | `jk2-sp`, `jk2-mp` | Experimental, dedicated server only: `-Djk2-development=true`. |
| American McGee's Alice | `alice` | Experimental, Windows x86: `-Dalice-development=true`. |
| Heavy Metal: F.A.K.K.2 | `fakk2` | Experimental, Windows x86: `-Dfakk2-development=true`. |
| Medal of Honor: Allied Assault | `mohaa` | Experimental, Windows x86: `-Dmohaa-development=true`. |
| Quake III Arena IHV Test | `q3-ihv` | Experimental, Windows x86 dedicated server only: `-Dq3-ihv-development=true`. |
| Quake III Arena Q3Test 1.02 to 1.08 | `q3test` | Experimental, Windows x86: `-Dq3test-development=true`. |

These ten games and sixteen profiles are the project's scope. **Full
compatibility is not yet established for any profile.** The
[Games Guide](docs/GAMES.md) says what each game does today, and the
[compatibility ledger](docs/fntech3/COMPATIBILITY.md) records the build, unit,
fixture, live and interoperability evidence behind it. A successful build or
an inherited FnQL feature does not close a runtime gate.

fnTech3 is engine-only. Supply a legitimate installation of the game you
select; its modules, assets and proprietary runtimes stay there. Release
packages exclude the optional proprietary Steam provider and Valve's Steam
runtimes. Steam services need a separately supplied compatible provider, and
services that are missing report themselves unavailable honestly.

## 2. Features

These capabilities come from the engine baselines and from fnTech3's own work.
Each applies to a game only where its guide says so, and each must still be
validated per profile against the compatibility ledger. Quake Live's WebUI,
Steam and Workshop services belong to the Quake Live profile alone.

- **Unified game compatibility** -- one immutable profile per process owns the
  game's installation rules, module interfaces, protocol, demos, saves, file
  formats and optional services. Adapters convert at every boundary, so one
  game's structures never reach another's modules or packets. RTCW and Elite
  Force change mode by relaunching the same executable.
- **Fast, modern hardware rendering** -- choose between optimized **GLx**,
  **Vulkan raster** and **Vulkan RTX** renderers in every game with a client,
  with GPU-cached world geometry, merged lightmaps, reduced rendering overhead
  and improved depth precision. RTX path traces each map with its own lights,
  under the light model of the compiler that built it.
- **Modern visual upgrades** -- optional HDR rendering and output, MSAA,
  supersampling, render scaling, anisotropic filtering, bloom, tone mapping,
  colour grading, per-pixel and shadowed dynamic lights, static map lights,
  motion blur, lens flares, soft particles, enhanced liquids, an underwater
  view and per-map fog.
- **Improved player visibility** -- optional player rim lighting, stencil
  outlines or both in Quake III Arena and Quake Live, with configurable
  enemy, teammate, team and free-for-all colours, intensity and outline
  thickness. Cel shading, model outlines and world-edge outlines are also
  available.
- **Better modern hardware and OS support** -- SDL3 video, input and audio,
  OpenAL, modern graphics APIs, high-resolution and high-refresh displays,
  borderless, windowed and fullscreen modes, fast Alt+Enter switching,
  controller hotplugging, and each game's own application icon, remade at high
  resolution.
- **Low-latency, configurable controls** -- raw mouse input, Quake
  Live-accurate CPI scaling, signed acceleration, sensitivity caps and
  angle-history filtering, while retaining classic Quake3e/ioquake3 mouse
  styles. Modern gamepads and Unicode text input are supported, and each
  game's menus see the key numbers and key names their original engine gave
  them.
- **Modern spatial audio** -- OpenAL with HRTF headphone positioning, surround
  output, environmental reverb, geometry-based occlusion, audio zones shipped
  for the maps of Quake III Arena, Quake Live, RTCW, Enemy Territory, Alice,
  F.A.K.K.2 and Medal of Honor, output-device selection, live device recovery and fallback to the
  classic software mixer. Each game's own sound distances, channels and loops
  feed the same pipeline; the software mixer remains the retail-exact option.
- **Major quality-of-life improvements** -- a scalable console in each game's
  own font, smooth scrolling, mouse selection, clipboard support,
  drag-and-drop editing, live command/cvar/map completion, widescreen FOV and
  HUD correction for every game written for 4:3 screens, minimize hotkeys,
  focus muting and lower background CPU usage.
- **Better screenshots, video and streaming tools** -- PNG, TGA, JPEG and BMP
  capture, clipboard screenshots, custom filenames, camera metadata,
  configurable levelshots, watermarks, cube-map capture, arbitrary-resolution
  output and FFmpeg video piping.
- **Faster content handling** -- quicker startup and map restarts with large
  PK3 libraries, raised filesystem and map limits, fast redirected downloads
  and, in Quake Live, automatic handling of server-required Workshop content.
- **Greater stability and security** -- extensive Quake3e and FnQuake3 fixes,
  bounded parsing of every game's files, demos and packets, lower memory use,
  stronger network protection, and graceful fallbacks when optional rendering,
  audio, WebUI or Steam functionality is unavailable.

## 3. Getting Started

Build from source with the [Build Guide](BUILD.md). Release packages are built
for Windows x86 and Linux x86 and start Quake III Arena and Quake Live; every
other game needs a developer build with its option. The shared client proof
target is Windows x86, because the retail game modules of Quake Live and of
every experimental game with a client are 32-bit Windows libraries. The Linux
i686 build is the dedicated-server target. Other platforms and architectures
are unsupported; the Q3-only x64 RTX diagnostic build does not establish the
shared contract.

Keep `fntech3-pkg.fnz`, `fnql-web.pak`, the `fnql_<renderer>_x86` modules and
the required redistributable libraries beside `${executable_basename}.exe` and
`${executable_basename}.ded.exe`. The engine package keeps each game's data
under its own `games/<profile>/<gamedir>/` folder. Upgrade the executable and
`fntech3-pkg.fnz` together; the old `FnQL-pkg.fnz` layout is no longer read.
See the [package layout](docs/fntech3/PACKAGES.md).

### Starting a game

Name the profile and the installation, and give each game its own writable
home. These examples run windowed:

```powershell
.\${executable_basename}.exe +set com_gameProfile quake3 +set fs_basepath "<Quake III Arena folder>" +set fs_homepath "<Q3 home>" +set r_fullscreen 0
.\${executable_basename}.exe +set com_gameProfile quakelive +set fs_basepath "<Quake Live folder>" +set fs_homepath "<QL home>" +set r_fullscreen 0
```

A developer build starts the experimental games the same way:

```powershell
.\${executable_basename}.exe +set com_gameProfile rtcw-sp +set fs_basepath "<Return to Castle Wolfenstein folder>" +set fs_homepath "<RTCW SP home>" +set r_fullscreen 0
.\${executable_basename}.exe +set com_gameProfile alice +set fs_basepath "<Alice folder>" +set fs_homepath "<Alice home>" +set r_fullscreen 0
```

Without `com_gameProfile`, the engine looks for exactly one recognized
installation in `fs_basepath`, beside the executable, then in the Steam
libraries. Two different games, or a game whose modes share one installation
-- Return to Castle Wolfenstein, Elite Force, Jedi Academy and Jedi Outcast --
need the profile named. The profile cannot change while the process runs; a
mode change relaunches the executable. `gameList` in the console lists every
profile and whether this build can start it. The [Games Guide](docs/GAMES.md)
covers detection, writable homes and each game in detail.

### Dedicated servers

The shared dedicated executable is `${executable_basename}.ded`. Use an explicit
profile and a separate home, for example on Linux:

```sh
./${executable_basename}.ded +set com_gameProfile quakelive +set fs_basepath "<Quake Live folder>" +set fs_homepath "<QL server home>" +set dedicated 2 +map campgrounds
```

Quake Live's Linux server runs the retail `qagamei386.so`; the native Linux
client cannot load Quake Live's Win32-only client and UI modules. Steam and the
WebUI remain optional Quake Live services and report unavailable services
honestly.

## 4. Documentation

The [documentation index](docs/README.md) lists everything. The main entries:

### Player guides

- [Games Guide](docs/GAMES.md) for the supported games and modes, choosing a
  profile, installations, where files are written, and each game's status.
- [Display Guide](docs/DISPLAY.md) for renderer selection, display modes, HDR,
  render scaling, anti-aliasing, dynamic and static lighting, shadows, fog,
  liquids, lens flares, motion blur and other presentation controls.
- [RTX Renderer Guide](docs/RTX.md) for ray-tracing requirements, path
  tracing, renderer selection, fallback behavior and troubleshooting.
- [GLx Renderer Guide](docs/GLX.md) for the default OpenGL-lineage renderer,
  its diagnostics and troubleshooting.
- [Visuals Guide](docs/VISUALS.md) for player highlighting, team and enemy
  colours, and outlines.
- [Aspect Handling Guide](docs/ASPECT_CORRECTION.md) for widescreen FOV, HUD
  layout rules, menus and cinematics.
- [Audio Guide](docs/AUDIO.md) for backends, devices, HRTF, spatial effects,
  audio zones, each game's sound, fallback behavior and troubleshooting.
- [Console Guide](docs/CONSOLE.md) for console layout and fonts, completion,
  clipboard use, mouse interaction, and notify and chat lines.
- [Screenshot Guide](docs/SCREENSHOTS.md) for capture commands, formats,
  filenames, metadata, levelshots, watermarks and cube maps.

### Technical and build docs

- [Build Guide](BUILD.md) for compiling fnTech3 and enabling each game's
  development option.
- [Architecture overview](docs/fntech3/ARCHITECTURE.md) for how profiles,
  adapters and the shared subsystems fit together.
- [Compatibility ledger](docs/fntech3/COMPATIBILITY.md) for the unified goal,
  all sixteen profiles, observed evidence, open gates and release sign-off.
- [Engine package](docs/fntech3/PACKAGES.md) for `fntech3-pkg.fnz` and its
  per-game sidecars.
- [Changelog](CHANGELOG.md) for fnTech3's reviewed release history, starting at
  0.1.0, and [releasing](docs/fntech3/RELEASING.md) for preparing a version.
- [Project mission and contributor rules](AGENTS.md) for the shared-engine
  invariants and non-regression requirements.
- [RTX Renderer Contract](docs/fnql/RTX_RENDERER.md) for the renderer's module
  boundary, conservative defaults, build gates and runtime validation.
- [FnQL baseline documents](docs/fnql/README.md) for the Quake Live profile's
  engine contracts -- [WebUI](docs/fnql/WEBUI_BACKEND.md),
  [Steam provider](docs/fnql/STEAM_PROVIDER.md),
  [input](docs/fnql/INPUT_COMPATIBILITY.md) and more -- and the inherited
  renderer and audio design notes.
- [FnQL baseline release history](https://github.com/themuffinator/FnQL/releases)
  for historical downloads and changelogs; these are not fnTech3 releases.

## 5. Credits

fnTech3 combines [FnQuake3](https://github.com/themuffinator/FnQ3) and
[FnQL](https://github.com/themuffinator/FnQL), with the id Software, ioquake3
and Quake3e engine lineage. QLSRP, OpenJK, OpenAlice, iortcw, Lilium Voyager
and other projects supply behavioral and format evidence for independently
written implementation work; each use is recorded in the credits.

See [CREDITS.md](CREDITS.md) for pinned source lineage, acknowledgements and
trademark notices, and [THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md](THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md) for
incorporated external implementation data and its license.
